config SYS_PROCESS
	bool "System-layer process services"
	depends on PROCESS
	select SYS_ELASTIPOOL
	imply SYS_HASH_MAP
	imply SYS_HASH_FUNC32
	help
	  Everything allocation-adjacent that kernel process support
	  deliberately does not own: integer process/group/session numbering
	  (sys_process_id() and friends), dynamic process records beyond the
	  kernel's static minimum, and the group/session cell pools backing
	  runtime k_setsid()/k_setpgid(). Without this option the kernel serves
	  statically defined processes only: records up to the built-in
	  minimum, build-time topology, no runtime group or session creation,
	  and no numeric IDs.

if SYS_PROCESS

config SYS_PROCESS_MIN
	int "Minimum size of the process record pool"
	default 4
	help
	  Minimum number of process records guaranteed to be allocatable for
	  k_spawn() children (including zombies not yet reaped), provided to
	  the kernel from a static pool in the system layer. Applications and
	  libraries contribute additional guaranteed records by defining int
	  symbols named CONFIG_SYS_PROCESS_MIN_ADD_<NAME>; the build system
	  sums this option with all such contributions and, for technical
	  reasons, provides the result to code as the un-prefixed
	  SYS_PROCESS_MIN compile definition.

config SYS_PROCESS_MAX
	int "Maximum number of heap-provided process records"
	default 0
	help
	  Number of process records that may be provided to the kernel from the
	  heap, in addition to the statically guaranteed minimum (the sum of
	  CONFIG_SYS_PROCESS_MIN and all CONFIG_SYS_PROCESS_MIN_ADD_*
	  contributions).
	  The kernel itself never allocates: records beyond the minimum come
	  from the system layer (lib/os), without guarantees, and require a
	  system heap. The default (0) disables dynamic process records
	  entirely.

config SYS_PROCESS_IDS
	int "Maximum number of simultaneously numbered processes"
	default 16
	help
	  Size of the system-layer numbering tables behind sys_process_id(),
	  sys_pgrp_id(), and sys_session_id(). Must cover the three reserved
	  processes plus every statically defined and runtime process that can
	  exist at once (including zombies not yet reaped).

endif # SYS_PROCESS
